# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# NOT-12: while a **full-screen page** is up, nothing reaches the
# program. `not-log-modal.leg`'s claim, for the screen M5-E4d (#305)
# added — and it is the same claim because it is the same situation: the
# party's own command-bar routine is live underneath, which is *why* the
# page may take the whole screen, so a key this seam left alone would
# pick a command, or walk the party, where nobody could see it, and the
# loop would paint its bar and its status line back over the page to
# prove it (#230).
#
# A **single** leg and not a pair, for `not-log-modal.leg`'s reason: the
# seam-off run has no page to swallow those keys, so every one of them
# reaches the program and the two runs end in different game states.
about nothing reaches the program while a full-screen page is up
kind single
program START.EXE
store tests/visual/reader-store.txt
# The listing has to have a row to open, and a citation is a place in the
# story: #301's cheat puts everything the store holds onto the log, which
# is what a leg needs and what a walk to the city hall would only test
# the walk of.
cite-all
both code-wheel
# The code-wheel bypass is gated on the wheel itself (#115), and this run
# drives past the challenge, so it needs it. Pinned by digest because a
# document never enters this tree.
document 0db301aeac4d2ec1e63b409ca6d3c9d39c63381d6712ff2ce7edc0528c6586fd
seam journal
until 300000000
every 25

# **A screen this seam draws is slow to appear, and the ranges below are
# spaced for it.** It is painted over successive arrivals, a few rows at
# a time, and the program's own drawing routines are what put each row
# on the glass. Measured by hand on the shorter boot (#305): a
# twenty-row page takes about 230 frames to settle after the key that
# opened it and a ten-row listing about 130. That is the listing's own
# behaviour since #222 rather than anything the page added; a `same`
# range that started sooner would be measuring the paint.

key A@7601
key Return@7651
key L@8951
key A@9201
# The listing, then the row the cursor is already on, then the barrage:
# search, cast, look, three the party walks by, and the letter the
# listing's own bottom row names — which is not a way out of a page.
key N@10600
key Return@11200
key S@11800
key C@12000
key L@12200
key Left@12400
key Right@12600
key Up@12800
# And then the way out, which `E` is: the page's own bar says `EXIT` and
# the letter of a word on a bar is how this game leaves every screen it
# has (M5-E4f, #317). It was swallowed with the rest of the barrage
# before that, so the `same` line below used to run past it; it stops
# short of it now. What the barrage is evidence for has not changed - six
# keys the adventuring screen would have answered, and a screen that does
# not move.
key E@13100

# From the page settling to the last of the barrage, one frame.
same 11500-13075
